In the group, the Polish rivals are the representatives of Spain, the Czech Republic, Azerbaijan and Moldova.
Group winners and the top three teams from the second places will advance directly to the final tournament.
The remaining six teams from the second positions will play in the barrages for the last three passes to the European Championships.
The stakes of 16 participants will be complemented by the host – England.
Poland's men's national team in FIFA's September rankings dropped from 20th to 22nd.
"Rz": Smog crushed the government
"The effects of "Clean air" are slim, and coal stoves will mine this winter as if nothing ever happened," reads the first page of today's "Rzeczpospolita."
“Until now, 82 thousand applications have been submitted under the Clean Air Programme for a total value of 2 billion zlotys, of which 48 thousand decisions were positive.
Accepted applications amount to a total of more than 915 million zlotys", says "Rz", citing data from the spokesperson of the Minister of the Environment.
As the newspaper points out, only part of the costs incurred are reimbursed from the program.
The results of the announced noisiness of the journal are assessed as "modest."
Local governments, quoted by “Rz”, are seeing the failure of “Clean Air” m.in. in too much bureaucracy.
“The program should be much simpler: electronic application, sending invoices by mail, possible random control of implementation.
This would improve the activities,” believes the mayor of Lidzbark Warmiński Jacek Wiśniowski.
Activists from the Polish Smog Alarm note that in the first year, less than 1 percent of the assumed target of replacing 3 million mounding boilers was achieved.
As “Rz” notes, although the budget of the program is 103 billion zlotys for 10 years, i.e. 10 billion zlotys per year, this amount is virtual.
"In the budget of the National Fund for Environmental Protection and Water Management 1.4 billion zlotys has been reserved for this year," it reads.
Congress agrees on F-35 for Poland
Błaszczak: Now price negotiations
The U.S. Congress has agreed to sell 32 of the state-of-the-art F-35 aircraft to Poland.
“This is one of the last steps before the conclusion of the contract, but it’s not the end of our work,” Mariusz Błaszczak wrote on Twitter.
“We will negotiate hard in order to achieve the best possible price,” the Polish minister added.
Contract for Polish F-35
Ultimately, the Polish air force will be powered by 32 F-35 Lightning II fighters manufactured by Lockheed Martin.
In mid-September, the U.S. State Department agreed to sell them.
The communiqué emphasized that the investment will support the national security and foreign policy of the United States by improving the security of the NATO ally, which is an important force for political stability in Europe.
According to the State Department, Poland is expected to pay 6.5 billion dollars for fighter jets.
However, this is not the final price.
The State Department pointed out that the contract will also include the supply of spare and repair parts, training equipment, the performance of engineering, logistics and staffing services, and related elements of logistics support and program and personnel training.
Lockheed Martin F-35 Fighters
The F-35 Lightning II fighter, produced by Lockheed Martin, is a single-seater and single-engine fifth-generation multi-purpose fighter.
The aircraft is capable of carrying out missions of close support, bombardment and typically fighter air combat tasks.
Thanks to stealth technology, the fighter is undetectable to short-range radars.
Recently, F-35 fighter jet jets bought Belgium as part of a contract worth $4.55 billion.
The Belgians want to replace them with the aging F-16.
Other U.S. allies, including Finland, Switzerland and the United Arab Emirates, are also expected to purchase U.S. combat aircraft.
Barbie against stereotypes.
Gender dolls
Barbie has launched a series of gender-neutral dolls.
That's how he wants to fight stereotypes.
Producer Barbie has launched a series of gender-neutral dolls on the market
Next to Barbie with different skin colors, figures or hairstyles, Mattel has introduced gender-neutral dolls for sale.
Toys from the “Creatable World” series are worn by wigs.
Therefore, children can decide whether their Barbie should have short hair or a long one.
Their wardrobe consists of both trousers and dresses.
In the blink of an eye, dolls can become boys and vice versa.
They are also available in different skin colors and with different clothes.
It was also abandoned to emphasize the distinctive sexual characteristics – gender dolls no longer have full lips, long eyelashes and a slim waist or – in Ken’s case – wide shoulders.
The company believes that children don't like it when their dolls have imposed gender norms, and some parents and feminists are convinced that toys made with a specific gender in mind hamper girls' ambitions and reinforce traditional stereotypes.
“New dolls will allow all children to feel free,” Mattel announces.
The Barbie doll, which appeared on the market in 1959 in swimsuits and high-heeled shoes, has in the past been repeatedly criticized for propagating "unhealthy female stereotypes," such as, for example, the unnatural body proportion.
The company reacted to criticism and has already launched dolls of different skin colors, as well as more rounded shapes.
There were also dolls in the hijab.
Zandberg: a circle or club Together can have more than a dozen MPs
Zandberg, when asked on the ZET radio how many candidates of his party can get to the Sejm from the lists of left-wing groups (KW SLD from whose list the candidates for the SLD, Spring and the Left Together, starting with the common “Left” logo), replied that it depends on the result of the committee.
“I believe that the result of the Left will be very good, because we feel that people associate hope with the fact that the Left is not only right, but it also has strength,” he said.
According to Zandberg, it is clear that the left can have 60–80 MPs.
“This may mean that a circle or club Together will have a dozen MPs.
But it depends on Poles.
“I won’t guess and talk about how it’s going to happen,” he said.
Asked if the Left Together would have a separate circle, Zandberg replied: “We are agreed that we are diverse, and we will not pretend that this diversity does not exist.
I think that this diversity was rewarded by Poles, because we showed that, differing, we were able to get along as to the common program.”
When asked whether the biggest beneficiary of the joint launch is SLD leader Włodzimierz Czarzasty, Zandberg replied that, in his opinion, the biggest beneficiaries are centre-left and left-wing voters.
On the other hand pointed out that the SLD will receive the entire subsidy, he replied that “the goal of the political party is to represent its constituents.”
Zandberg admitted that he will vote for himself in the elections to the Sejm.
In the event of an election to the Senate, he indicated that he was wondering.
“The offer is diverse and there are candidates and candidates to which my heart is closer, but there are also those to which my heart is further,” he said.
However, I will say very honestly and clearly that if I found myself in Constituency No. 44 of the Senate, I would take an electoral certificate and vote in another constituency," the politician stressed.
“I don’t think I’d be tempted to cast my vote for Minister Casimir Ujazdowski (Candidate of the KO - PAP),” he added.
Paweł Kasprzak and Marek Rudnicki from PiS compete in this district outside Ujazdowski.
Dakar: West Africa's largest mosque opened
Tens of thousands of people from all over Senegal gathered Friday in the capital for the inauguration of a huge mosque, believed to be the largest in West Africa.
Muslims came by buses, cars and on foot to the Bopp district, where a new mosque stood, able to accommodate 300 thousand faithful.
The building was built by the Mouride fraternity, belonging to the Sufi stream of Islam, which dominates Senegalus.
Work on the mosque began ten years ago in a wetland area of six hectares, donated by the government.
The name of Massalikul Jinan's Mosqué ("Paths to Paradise") comes from the title of Sheikh Ahmadou Bamba Mbacke, the 19th-century founder of the fraternity revered by adherents as a saint.
The mosque is covered with Karrarian marble and has five minarets.
The tallest of them is 78 meters.
The mosque inside accommodates 15,0000 faithful, and the other one can stay on the external esplanade.
In richly finished interiors you can admire the gold-plated dome, giant chandeliers and hand-made decorations made by workers from Morocco.
Builders say the mosque is the largest in West Africa, but the edifice has no comparison to mosques in other regions anyway.
In Morocco, Hassan II Mosque in Casablanca can accommodate 105,000 believers.
The construction of the mosque, which cost more than 30 million euros, was financed from private donations.
In addition to the land, the government funded lighting, sanitary facilities and financed road works worth 10.5 million euros.
Coach Smyła: Our problem is not Silesia, but ourselves
A streak of eight league matches without a win, Crown players will try to stop in Friday’s meeting with the vice-leader of the Silesian Extraclass.
“Our problem is not Wroclaw, it’s us, we have to work on what we’re playing,” emphasized Kielczan coach Mirosław Smyła.
The last and so far the only victory in the league was recorded in the first round, when on the trip they defeated 1:0 Benjamin of Raków Częstochowa.
Later, Kielce recorded two draws and six defeats, and on Tuesday they lost 0:1 to Zagłębie Lubin in 1/32 of the final of the Polish Cup.
“I’m optimistic that in the last seconds of the knockout game, the players fought for the goal, they wanted to draw.
Their fall on the turf after the final whistle did not result from the breakdown, only it was a fall of anger because they were so close," Smyła said.
As he pointed out, Silesia has very strong wings, which is why it will sensitize the team to play in the side sectors of the pitch.
“We’re aware of your opponent’s quality, but we’re also looking at what we want to play.”
In Friday's match in the Crown will certainly not be played pausing for the excess of yellow cards Ognjen Gnjatic, and Jakub Żubrowski and Michael Gardawski will also be injured in the frame.
On the other hand, it is exciting to report in a knockout match of several players with the desire to play.
"We have a headache, recently it seemed that it would not be, and now it is," Smyła stated.
Defender of the Crown Daniel Dziwniel emphasized that Silesia will not be an easy rival to break the streaks of eight league matches in a row without a victory, while in an extraclass anyone can win against anyone.
“We’re a good team.
In recent weeks, we may not show it on the pitch, but there will come a moment when we will win and we believe that it will be on Friday," he said.
After nine queues of extra-class, the Crown with the achievements of five points ranks 15th.
Silesia has accumulated 12 more and ranks second.
Friday's match in Kielce will start at 20.30.
These trees can completely disappear from Europe
"42 per cent of the tree species that make up Europe's stands are at risk," the International Union for Conservating Nature (IUCN) warned in a "Red book of European trees" published on Friday.
At the top of the list are m.in. chestnuts and rowans.
IUCN, an organisation headquartered in Gland, Switzerland, publishes a list of endangered species of European trees for the first time.
It was prepared by specialists who, on the basis of the research, came to the conclusion that out of 454 species of trees found in Europe (as well as elsewhere in the world), 42 percent are "highly endangered."
Among the causes, the Red Paper lists the rapid growth of urban agglomerations and global climate change.
However, those trees and shrubs that occur only or mainly on our continent are the most endangered.
When it comes to a typical European stand, according to IUCN specialists, 58 percent of species are endangered, and 15 percent, i.e. 66 species of trees and shrubs, are in danger of extinction.
Among the main causes of deforestation, the Red Book lists errors in the operation of the stand, accelerated urbanization, excessively intensive felling of trees, followed by forest fires and changes taking place in the European ecostystemie.
According to the authors of the report, the existence of many European varieties of shrubs, mosses and lichens is also strongly endangered.
"The consequences of human activity lead to the gradual disappearance of European plant species and pose in Europe the danger of the extinction of important and underestimated varieties, which form the basis of European ecosystems and have an impact on the health of our planet," concluded the Luc Bas report, which directs the IUCN's European office.
Grzegorz Schetyna: I don't promise 600+
“Today PiS wants to bribe Poles by offering them for freedom to give away for a loan, which will have to repay future generations,” says Grzegorz Schetyna in an interview with “Rzeczpospolita”.
In his opinion, “the whole policy of PiS is a pic”.
At the same time, the politician promises that "what is given will not be taken."
Schetyna assures Jacek Nizinkiewicz that she has an idea to defeat the Law and Justice.
It's about mobilising the electorate at the ballot box.
In his opinion, the opposition has more potential in this regard than Kaczynski's party.
“Today, conscious people who want to live in a normal state, there are more than supporters of PiS, a party that has defied the values proclaimed, such as modesty, humility, transparency,” says Schetyna.
The leader of the Civic Coalition and the chairman of the Civic Platform also emphasized the basic assumptions of the KO electoral programme.
“First of all, nothing given will be received, but you have to set a direction for the future, because PiS deals only with the situation here and now, there is no plan for Poland, which can be seen even from dramatically low investments,” he said.
Secondly, it is about “launching the energy of Poles, helping and investing in those who work reliably – and those who are entrepreneurs”.
Schetyna explains that the KO program included a m.in. project, which will give 614 zlotys more on the hand, and more earners – also several hundred.
The politician also pointed out that he did not want to race for a giveaway.
As he said, he'll do as much as he can, but he doesn't promise 600+.
“I will never allow the Greek variant, where the rulers were bidding with the opposition for a giveaway, until the financial collapse of the country finally occurred,” he stressed.
Schetyna in an interview with “Rz” also stated that Jarosław Kaczyński and Mateusz Morawiecki are afraid to debate with him and Małgorzata Kidawa-Błońska.
“We would expose their hypocrisy and propaganda,” he said.
“Pride walks before the fall.
Kaczynski is such a statesman as Banaś is crystal," he added.
The PO leader also said that he has not yet decided whether he will apply for Platform chairmanship.
Argentina: Six dead condors of the great
In the Argentinian province of Santa Cruz, six dead condors of the great have been found, TVN24 reports.
Experts believe that human activity has contributed to their deaths.
Large condors have probably been poisoned with a means to kill predators such as cougas, foxes or wild dogs.
The animals may have eaten toxic carcass.
In addition to one young individual, the rest of the dead condors were grown-up.
“It’s a big loss for the environment, because the condor has to reach 10–15 years for it to become fertile and able to reproduce.
So we're losing a protected species, an endangered species," says Luis Jacome, director of the BioAndina Foundation, an Argentine environmental organization.
“I think the use of toxic bait and poison puts not only this species in danger, but all other forms of life, including human health.”
"Rz": They want a jewel for the president
Work has begun on awarding the head of the state to the precious chain.
The idea was supported by Andrzej Duda’s office – we read in “Rzeczpospolita”.
According to the newspaper, it is about the golden chain of the Order of the White Eagle, the royal insignium officially called in the interwar period the jewel of the Commonwealth.
The last time it was used probably by President Ignacy Mościcki.
“Rz” reports that soon such a chain may hit the shoulders of Andrzej Duda.
The Senate Committee on Human Rights, the Rule and Petitions on Wednesday passed a motion to work on an amendment to a bill on orders and decorations in order to bring it back.
The newspaper writes that this is also not the first attempt to restore this chain to the head of state.
The Senate had already proposed such an amendment in 1992, but it was lost with one vote.
This time, the work is quick.
At the beginning of August, the senators devoted a few minutes to this matter, recognizing that they would seek the opinion of the President's Office.
This came in mid-September.
“I kindly inform you that the Chancellery of the President of the Republic of Poland does not submit comments to the position presented in this petition,” wrote Andrzej Dera at the time.
On Wednesday, the matter was continued by the Senate committee and again devoted little time to the matter.
“The President’s Office replied briefly to us: it is in favour of restoring it,” said the head of the committee, Robert Mamątow from PiS, quoted by “Rz.”
He submitted a request for legislative work, which the committee supported.
Police will teach drivers how to drive through an intersection
The police action will be conducted m.in. at the intersection of Cieplińskiego Avenue with Piłsudskiego Avenue.
The actions of the uniforms have something to do with the recent complaints of the city authorities against drivers who are not always able to behave appropriately at the intersection.
On Friday, September 27, police officers start preventive actions “Passable intersections” in Rzeszów.
It is about eliminating obstacles in traffic: entering the intersection without the possibility of exit, failure to comply with light signals or traffic signs.
“We often think that we are alone at the intersection, and in fact we make life difficult for other drivers,” Marek Ustrobiński, the city’s vice-president, said a few days ago.
His words resounded at a press conference of the city hall, at which the city authorities presented examples of inappropriate movement of cars at intersections, which in effect creates traffic jams.
At that time, the president appealed to travelers for sense on the road and m.in. faster moving at traffic lights or non-writing texts while driving.
The topic was taken over by Rzeszów policemans, who from today are starting the action "Passable intersections".
What's going on, huh?
The aim of the actions is to eliminate the phenomenon of reducing passability at intersections by drivers entering them without the possibility of continuing driving.
It's a common offense that contributes to intensifying obstructions in movement and creating traffic jams.
It is threatened with a mandate of 300 zlotys and 2 penalty points.
In addition, the uniforms will pay attention to non-compliance with traffic light signals or signs and incorrect parking in the area of intersections.
They will also observe the lack of driving fluidity, late starting from under the beacons, slow crossings and leaving too many distances between vehicles.
At what intersections will activities be carried out?
M.in. on Lisa-Kuli Street from Jagiellońska Street; Cieplińskiego Avenue from Piłsudskiego Avenue; Targowa Street from Piłsudskiego Avenue; Śreniawitów Square from Dąbrowskiego Street or Hetmańska Street from Warsaw Powstańców Avenue.
Such actions in the Podkarpacie capital will be conducted on the next Fridays of October.
They inspected a company that disposes of waste from “Czajka”
“Officials of the Department of Environmental Protection of the Wołomiński County and the District Building Surveillance Inspectorate inspected on Friday an installation in the commune of Zielonka, where waste from the “Czajka” wastewater treatment plant in Warsaw is disposed of,” said Karol Szyszko, a spokesperson of the Wołomiński district.
Inspectors from the Wołomiński district carried out inspections in a plant located in forests belonging to Zielonka commune.
Under the agreement with the capital’s wastewater treatment plant “Czajka”, the “Czajka” is carried out by wastewater management services.
“The inspection of the installation was carried out, in addition, the entrepreneur was asked to provide documentation related to his activities,” said Szyszko.
He added that the follow-up report will be forwarded m.in. to the Provincial Inspectorate of Environmental Protection in Warsaw.
The last inspection in “Dar-Trance” was carried out by the Provincial Inspectorate of Environmental Protection in Warsaw in the period 29 December 2015 – 11 February 2016.
It was then determined that the company did not carry out samples, measurements and groundwater tests, and did not have the results of tests of stabilized municipal sewage sludge from the “Czajka” wastewater treatment plant.
After the inspection of the WIOŚ, Dariusz Sobota was summoned to stop violating the conditions of the decision.
Dariusz Sobota is one of the companies collecting waste in the form of stabilized municipal sewage sludges from the “Czajka” treatment plant.
According to the decision to carry out waste disposal in the excavation, waste weighing up to 60 000 tons per year is processed in the forests near Zielonka.
The Warsaw Municipal Water and Sewerage Company reported last Thursday that at the turn of November and December 2018 STUOŚ was excluded from operation, which was related to the damage to one of the incineration plant equipment.
According to MPWiK, the monthly cost of their exports is 1.7 million zlotys (calculated on the basis of export costs from 01.01.2019 to 31.08.2019).
Lech Wałęsa supported Małgorzata Kidawa-Błońska.
"You have taken on a difficult task, you can count on my total support," Lech Wałęsa told Małgorzata Kidawa-Błońska on Friday.
“Mr. President, these are very important words for me,” replied the candidate of the Civic Coalition to the prime minister, the leader of the KO list in Warsaw.
The former president emphasized that when it comes to the situation in Poland, he is now “only a supporter in retirement”.
Małgorzata Kidawa-Błońska met on Friday with former President Lech Wałęsa in his office at the Gdańsk European Solidarity Centre.
Wałęsa’s son Jarosław also took part in a short conversation behind closed doors.
"You undertook a difficult task," Lech Wałęsa said after the meeting.
“You can count on my total support,” he added.
“Problems before Poland are known to all of us.
That is why it is a great hope that you, as a woman, will cope with these problems, and all of us – those who love Poland – will support your actions as much as possible," said the former president, turning to Kidawa-Błońska.
Very important words
Kidawa-Błońska thanked for her support.
“Mr. President, these are very important words for me, because none like you can talk about freedom, about the constitution, and know how important it is,” she said.
“Let’s show that you can make a different, wise policy, together, but according to the constitution, according to the law, according to respect for others.
This is how you did your revolution, and this is how we want to change Poland," emphasized Kidawa-Błońska.
Fan in retirement
Walesa told reporters that he was now "just a supporter in retirement."
“Of course I’m looking at it.
I hope that we will reverse the course of events, that what was good, proven, (for this) we will return.
The separation of powers is the most important, the constitution is the most important, the courts are absolutely necessary.
And all this needs to be cleared, all this needs to be restored to normal operation and I believe that this time we will do it.
Because if not, then we have a street criterion," said Lech Wałęsa.
“That’s why it’s so important for Poles to go to elections, think and make a decision on their own.
And this is the most important thing," added Kidawa-Błońska.
"The last chance that the card will improve the state of affairs that is in Poland," the former president said.
“We have to build the foundation again”
When asked about the most important changes that should take place in Poland, Kidawa-Błońska said: “The president said very well.
We need to rebuild the foundation on which we can do all other things.
Since the separation of powers does not exist, since the constitution is not respected, we need to go back to the beginning and lead to make it obvious and for everyone to know that otherwise Poland will not step forward.”
ME volleyball players: Slovenia – Poland 3:1 in the semi-finals.
On hot terrain in Ljubljana, Polish volleyball players clashed with the Slovenians in the semi-finals of the European Championships.
There was no shortage of controversy and discussion with the referees, with Polish coach Vital Heynen in the lead role.
“White-Reds” lost 1:3 and will play for the bronze medal.
The Senate has decided on key bills
The Senate passed without amendments a bill on the disclosure of the estates of the families of the most important civil servants and on the flights of the most important people in the state.
52 senators voted in favor of the amendment to the law on the assets of the families of civil servants – without amendments – voted, 14 were against, three abstained.
Thus, the senators decided to reject 12 amendments tabled during Wednesday’s debate by Senator Sławomir Rybicki.
According to him, the amendments were intended to "civilize" and limit the scope of the information given in the statement about the property situation of their own children, the spouse's children, children adapted to cases in which the children remain in the common household with the person obliged to make a statement.
The law on the transparency of the assets of the families of the most important civil servants, whose project was created in the chancellery of the prime minister, was adopted by the Sejm on September 11.
The regulations assume that m.in. prime ministers, ministers, MPs, MEPs, senators, presidents: the Constitutional Court, the Supreme Administrative Court and the Supreme Court, as well as persons at the head of other state institutions will be obliged to include in the property statement information about the personal property of spouses, children and people living together.
The Senate also did not propose amendments to the law on flights of the most important people in the state.
It regulates what flights have HEAD status and who may be on board government planes along with the most important people in the state.
The law also specifies who is responsible for maintaining the Central Flight Register.
55 senators voted in favour of the bill, 12 were against, four abstained.
According to the bill, any flight of the president will be a flight "performed in an official mission."
On the flights of the president will be able to be accompanied by family members and others invited by the president.
On the other hand, the flight in the official mission of the Prime Minister and the Marshals of the Sejm and the Senate will be only the flight “held in order to carry out tasks directly related to its function”.
The law also specifies the list of people who may accompany the most important people in the country during the flight.
According to the regulations, there may be members of delegations – invited Members, senators, representatives of public authorities and experts; persons providing technical, medical or organizational services, as well as journalists.
According to the law, in the event of doubts related to the flights of the Marshals of the Sejm and the Senate, the head of the KPRM will apply for their clarification.
If such doubts are not clarified within the allotted time, the head of the KPRM will be obliged to notify the heads of the office of the Sejm or the Senate about the inability to organize such a flight.
