A graduate of the Academy of Physical Education in Biała Podlaska since 2002 he has been an instructor at the State University of Technology and Economics in Jarosław, and for 15 years he has been president
The Poles, after the first training in Paris, are waiting for their last rival
Polish volleyball players held their first training session in Paris on Friday, where they will appear in the match for a bronze medal on Saturday.
Vital Heynen's ward the road to winning the title was closed by Thursday's defeat against the Slovenians 1:3 in Ljubljana.
It was loud about their hassles with traveling to this duel.
This time it was without such problems – on Friday morning they went together with the Slovenians charter to Paris, and a few hours later they held a training session in the famous Bercy hall.
According to the accounts of those present in the classes, the coach of world champions was annoyed.
As Heynen’s assistant Michał Mieszko Gogol pointed out, this time the main task was not to just practice volleyball elements.
“We went to the hall, but for the vast majority it was time for the team.
It was about spending it more together than training.
to talk, to think about how to rebuild all this as a team," he recounted.
He confirmed that the semi-final defeat was a painful lesson for both players and for the training staff.
“Were brutally brought down to earth.
Without a doubt, we are disappointed.
We still believed that we would go back to this game again.
Were discussing this match with each other on the staff, the coach talked a lot with the players.
As the two main reasons for the defeat, I would point out the inefficiency in the attack and much worse than usually dealing with situational balls.
The Slovenians were much better in this respect," he analyzed.
In his opinion, “White-Red” did not stand the stress, related to the performance against the high-profile and large-scale audience co-hosts of the tournament.
He acknowledged that the world champions might have put down a slightly smooth road to the semi-finals.
“Perhaps, if there had been a defeat in a similar style in the group stage, we would have analyzed this lesson more quickly and we would have played differently later in the later stages of the tournament.
But it's just if," he stipulated.
Gogol hopes that the anger associated with Thursday’s defeat of the Poles will manage to turn into aggression in the bronze match.
“I hope that we will show our true face.
There's something to fight about.
We recently won the ME medal in 2011," he recalled.
Paris is the fifth city, and France is the third country visited by Poles during these championships.
In the group stage, they competed in Rotterdam and Amsterdam, for the first two meetings of the group phase they went to Apeldoorn, and they moved to Ljubljana for the semi-final.
Nick Kyrgios: severe punishment, but suspended
The Australian tennis player received a 16-week ban on starts and a fine of 25 thousand dollars – in a six-month suspension – for reprehensible words, gestures and deeds during recent ATP tournaments.
ATP World Tour activists lose patience with Kyrgios, but they are still giving him a chance to improve.
A summary of the vices of the most uncouth professional tennis player of recent years looks dangerous – 16 weeks break plus a large fine, but both penalties are halted for six months – a trial period in which a wayward athlete will be able to improve.
Nick Kyrgios is only 24 years old, but he's been struggling for a long time and he've earned a kind of tennis world record -- he's had to pay the tallest-in-a-year-old single financial penalty for breaking the rules of behavior on the court and alongside him.
Loud curses, strange gestures, unsporty behavior, offending the referee and spectators, breaking two rackets during a losing match against Karen Khachanov – for all this after the August ATP Masters 1000 tournament in Cincinnati paid 113 thousand dollars.
In the 2019 U.S. Open, he didn’t play for long either, but he was able to publicly say that ATP activists “are corrupt”, which hurt the organization’s authorities so much that they implemented a disciplinary investigation against Kyrgios and after a few weeks gave the aforementioned verdict about the suspension of starts and another fine.
Words about corruption, however, were forgivened, after listening to the accused and getting to know the context of his speech.
However, in the justification, the tennis activists stated that the investigation showed that Kyrgios’ behavior for the past 12 months in many cases raised serious objections and constituted an almost continuous violation of the rules, that he constantly insulted people associated with tournaments and spectators in a vulgar way, hence the high penalty.
However, the ATP gave him five days to bring an appeal and immediately postponed the execution of the judgment, ordering the fulfillment of several conditions in the next six months: in the ATP Tour and ATP Challenge Tour Kyrgios tournaments, he can no longer in any way violate the rules of the code of conduct of professional tennis players, in particular he can no longer curse, destroy rackets, argue with judges and the audience, or any other way to behave indecently
He was also advised to look for “continuous support” from mental coaches during tournaments and also in the off-season to consult people specializing in solving the problems of athletes who cannot cope with proper behavior.
Nick Kyrgios's first comment (on social media) for the verdict was this: "Hey everyone, I can still play.
I just have to control my behavior, that’s all.”
In practice, the Australian will have at least a two-week break anyway, because his shoulder injured in the Lavera Cup, so he had to give up the upcoming starts in Beijing (September 30 – October 6) and Shanghai (6–13 October).
Kyrgios ranks 27th in the ATP rankings.
This year he won two tournaments – in Washington and Acapulco, he earned more than 1.3 million dollars, so even after the fines he will have a lot left.
GUS: We are getting better in Poland
According to a report published by the Central Statistical Office on household budgets published on Friday, 44 percent of them assessed as a good or rather good financial situation last year.
A year earlier, this percentage was 37.2 per cent, 33.5 per cent in 2016, and earlier – from 2009, when the statistical office first examined subjective assessments of the situation of households – never exceeded 30 percent.
A total of 8.1 per cent of households were considered to be bad or rather bad in their financial situation last year, compared to 11.2 per cent a year earlier and more than 20 percent in 2009.
The best is the financial situation of self-employed households.
In this group, more than 70 percent of respondents consider it good or rather good.
At the other pole are pensioner households, where the percentage of such responses was only 18 percent.
The subjective assessment of the financial situation of households is also greatly influenced by the number of children.
32 percent declare a good or rather good material situation to parents raising offspring alone, of which 32 percent declare a good or rather good material situation.
Among couples with children, this percentage ranges (depending on the number of children) from 55 to 60 percent, and among childless households is 48.8 percent.
In these statistics, the effect of the 500+ program is clearly visible.
Before it was launched (in 2016), the worst financial situation was judged by parents raising offspring alone and large families (with at least three children).
Improving subjective assessments of the financial situation of Poles is a reflection of a rapid increase in income, primarily from work.
However, this is not all, because the percentage of households, which consider their situation to be good, increases faster than real income.
Moreover, at the level of voivodships, there is no clear relationship between the level of income and the assessment of the material conditions of households.
The best assessments are made by the inhabitants of the Opole Voivodeship, where 48.9 percent of respondents consider their situation to be good or rather good.
Subsequently are Lubuskie (47.7 percent), Śląskie (47.6 percent), Masovian (47.3 percent) and Zachodniopomorskie (47.1 percent).
Meanwhile, in Opole, disposable income (i.e. after paying taxes and contributions) amounted to less than 94 percent of the national average last year, and in Lubuskie 98 percent.
The highest incomes were given to the inhabitants of Mazovian (120 percent of the national average) and Silesian average (104 per cent).
On average in Poland, disposable income per person amounted to 1693 zł in 2018.
In real terms (after a correction for the impact of price changes) increased by 4.3 per cent compared to 2017.
Compared to previous years, this is quite low.
In 2016, income per person increased by 7 per cent, and a year later by 6.3 percent.
Although the increase in income slows, it also inhibits the increase in spending.
Those per person amounted to 1187 zlotys last year, which means that in real terms decreased by 0.7 percent.
This is the first such case since 2013.
In 2017, spending per person increased by 1.9 per cent in reality, and by 4.3 percent in 2016.
Studies of household budgets suggest that a statistical household currently spends 70.1 percent of its disposable income, while in 2014 this share exceeded 80 percent, and by 2006 it exceeded 90 percent.
Paulina Smaszcz-Kurzajewska in a see-through blouse
Paulina Smaszcz-Kurzajewska appeared at the closed show of the brand “Patrizia Pepe”.
Unfortunately, her styling was not successful.
Paulina Smaszcz-Kurzajewska is the wife of sports journalist Maciej Kurzajewski
Paulina Smaszcz-Kurzajewska is a Polish journalist and presenter.
She is involved in activities for women, for whom she organizes interactive workshops, motivational lectures, lectures and conference panels.
For 25 years she worked in the public relations industry with companies such as PZU, Red Bull or Bayer.
As a journalist she worked in TVP1, TVN Style, Vision Sport and TVP2.
The woman is the author of the book "Mom and Dad is us", in which she talks about the health, development and nutrition of children from 0 to 3 years old.
She was involved in many projects that were aimed at providing support for professionally active women.
The activities managed by her include “Entrepreneurial Women’s Circle”, “Women’s Beacon”, “Women’s Congress Association” or “Make Up Buisness”.
Privately, she is the wife of Maciej Kurzajewski – a TV presenter and sports journalist.
The couple have two sons: Francis and Julian.
Paulina Smaszcz-Kurzajewska appeared in a closed show of the Italian brand "Patrizia Pepe".
Natasza Urbańska, Katarzyna Figura and Beata Chmielowska Olech also went to the event.
An intimate display could not take place without a wall, on which the stars boasted about their stylizations.
Unfortunately, photojournalists picked up Paulina Smaszcz-Kurzajewska's glaring error.
The woman wore a white bra under a black shirt, which was heavily see-through it.
U.S.: Three suicides among aircraft carrier's crew
Three sailors committed suicides aboard the U.S. George H. W. Bush last week, the U.S. Navy said.
Over the past two years, a total of five crew members of this ship have been killed.
The previous suicide took place in July this year, and earlier in November 2017.
According to CNN, all three sailors shot themselves last week.
Each of the incidents occurred on land when the U.S. George H. W. Bush moored off the coast of the state of Virginia.
So far, third-party involvement has been ruled out in the case.
In addition, deaths should not be related to each other.
“It is necessary for everyone to get involved in presenting suggestions and ideas on how we can prevent another suicide.
"I want to stress that there is no stigma or other repercussions related to the search for help," Captain Sean Bailey, the ship's commander, wrote on Facebook.
Niesiołowski critical of the Pope.
John Paul II didn't do anything
In an interview with Piotr Lekszycki, Stefan Niesiołowski emphasized that in 1981 John Paul II was a great authority for opposition activists.
“Actually, his every opinion was accepted.”
Later, there was criticism of some of his actions.
“For me the fact that John Paul II was unable to eliminate the harmful activity of Rydzyk, he puts a great shadow on his pontificate.
After all, this redemptorist – a pest of the Church and Poland – developed his media into the best during the life of the Pope,” he explained.
The former PO politician admitted: “there were squints of dissatisfaction, the pope said something, once Rydzyka did not accept anything, but he had power and for politicking he could simply throw him out of the Church or sent him on a mission to New Caledonia or Fiji to raise money for the poor there.
However, John Paul II did not do it, so did his successors," Niesiołowski explained.
“Besides, there has been this disastrous matter of pedophilia and the actions of the Church, and actually their absence.
I've run a little forward, but I wanted to draw attention to the whole of his life.
We have known about the scale of pedophilia in the Church relatively recently.
The question arises, how is it possible that John Paul II, being the head of the Church, did not know about these disgusting matters," Niesiołowski said in an interview.
In the opinion of the former OP politician, the Polish Church is an ally of PiS.
“It must be acknowledged that during the period of Solidarity the Church was our most important ally.
All the celebrations began with the Holy Mass, there were crosses everywhere.
Now these crosses are written, they have become a symbol of dictatorship – a sign of the written regime.
I repeat: one of the biggest unpleasant surprises is that such people as Rydzyk are shaking the Polish Church, that John Paul II did nothing in this matter and that today the Church is an ally of PiS.
It is a black chapter of the Polish Church, for which he will have to pay dearly,” argued Niesiołowski.
“I hope that Pope Francis will chase Jędraszewski, Rydzyk and a few others of this type,” he concluded.
