Voting on the break in deliberations during the Conservative Party conference
Britain's MPs voted against a three-day hiatus in the House of Commons deliberations for an annual Conservative Party programme conference, something the government has requested.
There were 289 MPs in favour, 306 against.
It is customary that the House of Commons does not meet during party conferences, so that deputies do not have to resign either from participation in them or from parliamentary meetings.
However, opposition parties have acknowledged that at such a crucial time as the final weeks ahead of Britain's scheduled exit from the European Union on October 31, parliament should not interrupt deliberations.
289 MPs voted in favour of the government’s request for a break, 306 were opposed.
Two opposition parties after the conventions
Failure to allow a break could be perceived as the political vengeance of the opposition on Prime Minister Boris Johnson for his five-week decision (on Tuesday, the decision was deemed unlawful and non-existent by the Supreme Court) and for the fact that she refuses to apologize for it after the judges' ruling.
Following this ruling, the House of Commons resumed the session on Wednesday, and the most important event of the day was Johnson's speech and the following turbulent debate.
Johnson accused the opposition of blocking Brexit and avoiding early elections.
“It’s time to implement Brexit.
It must be implemented in order to respect the result of the referendum.
Brexit must come so that we can address a deal on citizens' priorities, on health care, on the cost of living.
Brexit needs to be realised so that we can unite this country," Johnson said.
The Conservative Party conference is scheduled to begin in Manchester next Sunday and last until Wednesday 2 October.
Conferences of the two main opposition groups – the Labour Party and the Liberal Democrats – have already taken place.
One of the justifications that Johnson gave explaining such a lengthy suspension of parliament was precisely party conferences.
Andrzej Duda blames Israel for anti-Semitic attacks in Poland
“President Andrzej Duda believes that anti-Semitic attacks in Poland are a reaction to the anti-Polish rhetoric of Israeli leaders,” foreign media write.
A spokesperson for the president strongly denied the reports.
"This is simply not true," Błażej Spychalski tweeted.
Israeli media, m.in. “Haaretz” and “Jerusalem Post”, citing “Jewish Insider”, write that Andrzej Duda during a meeting with representatives of the Jewish diaspora at the consulate of the Republic of Poland in New York stated that it was Israel who was to blame for the recent anti-Semitic attacks in Poland.
The president said the increase in anti-Semitic attacks in Poland is a reaction to anti-Polish rhetoric of Israeli leaders.
He referred to the words of Israeli Foreign Minister Israel Katz, who stated in February that "Poles have sucked anti-Semitism with breast milk."
Andrzej Duda said that he regrets that Israel did not apologize for the humiliating statements of its politicians.
He also mentioned an act of aggression against Poland’s ambassador to Israel Marek Magierowski.
In May, he was attacked by an attacker who shouted: "Pole, Pole."
“Quote isn’t just inaccurate.
It's simply not true," Błażej Spychalski tweeted.
He emphasized that Duda "has never said that Israel is responsible for recent anti-Semitic attacks in Poland."
According to Spychalski, the portal “invented it.”
According to “JI” interlocutors, during the meeting there was also a dispute between the Holocaust survivor, awarded the Commander’s Cross of the Order of Merit of the Republic of Poland Edward Mosberg and Rabbi Shmuley Boteach.
Mosberg was supposed to agree with the words of President Duda and to say that it was Jews from Israel "such as Israel Kac" who are responsible for an increase in anti-Semitism.
According to Boteach sources, Mosberg's statement was supposed to be interrupted.
Who's Banaś and what's his fault?
The tenement house rented by pimps led the journalist to discover negligence in Marian Banaś's property statements.
We're summarizing another scandal that shocked the ruling camp.
“Superwizjera” TVN journalists revealed that Marian Banaś, the president of the Supreme Chamber of Control, in recent years did not enter into the property statement of the 400-meter tenement house, which is located in Krakow’s Podgórze.
What's more, his sub-tenant was supposed to run in the aforementioned tenement house sex business (i.e. rent rooms for hours).
On the other hand, the building itself has allegedly secured the repayment of the loan since 2016.
Bertold Kittel pointed to irregularities in "Supervisor."
The journalist revealed that from Banaś the tenement house was rented by people connected to the criminal world to use it as a place of "erotic meetings."
Clients were supposed to be men using the services of prostitutes.
Banaś was vaguely and murky about the accusations.
Interestingly, the tenement house hired by pimps led the journalist to discover negligence in Banasia's property statements.
The tenement house itself was vaguely described in them, and the income from its renting was to be underestimated.
“The property of Marian Banaś and his wife may be worth from 6 to 8 million PLN.
In total, the spouses can boast of two houses, three apartments, savings [...]”, writes Money.pl.
According to the analysis of the portal, Banasia’s tenement house alone was already worth about 3 million zlotys.
The Central Anti-Corruption Bureau is currently auditing Marian Banaś’s financial statements for the years 2015–2019.
Banaś announced that, by the time of its completion, he would suspend his activities in NIK. “I will apply to the marshal for unpaid leave,” he assured.
The opposition is calling for clarification.
“We don’t hear the CBA expand its investigation or speed up Marian Banaś’s examination of the property statements.
Therefore, we are forced to serve the authorities of the state and submit a notification about the possibility of committing a crime [...]” – said Mariusz Witczak, an MP.
"If a normal Pole had priced the rental of his properties so blatantly low, he would already have 10 inspections, a tax addition and criminal and fiscal proceedings," he added.
He's gone high, he's fallen low
Marian Banaś is the new president of the Supreme Audit Office.
He was appointed to this position on August 30, 2019, for a six-year term.
His candidacy was submitted by Law and Justice and by the votes of the party's politicians adopted.
Previously, Banaś was associated with NIK in the years 1992–2005, he acted in this institution as a collaborator of Lech Kaczyński.
During the first rule of PiS (2005–2008), Banaś held the position of the president of the Customs Service, as well as undersecretary of state in the Ministry of Finance.
Since 2015, he has again sat on the stool of the head of the Customs Service, and since 2017 he has managed the National Revenue Administration.
Banaś served for 3 months as Minister of Finance in the government of Mateusz Morawiecki.
Cooler Master introduces a handle for graphics cards to the market
Graphics cards can weigh a lot nowadays, and this naturally makes room for the category of products, which it would be vain to look for on store shelves until a decade ago.
Such a solution - a holder for the ELV8 RGB graphics card - was presented by Cooler Master.
The new handle model for the graphics card is not only a functional solution that allows you to maintain the graphics card more confidently without relying solely on the durability of the PCI-express slot.
Cooler Master ELV8 RGB also has an additional task – decorative.
"Rzeczpospolita": Ignoring the policeman's order will be punished
According to the newspaper, the disregard of an injunction issued by a police officer is intended to be a new type of misdemeanor, threatened with a fine, arrest or restriction of liberty.
Such a change is wanted by police trade unionists.
The idea went to the parliamentary Committee on Petitions.
“The case is serious, because already every year more than half a million offence cases go to Polish courts,” it reads.
“Rzeczpospolita” has determined that the petition, which the Independent Police Union, writes to MPs regarding the amendment of the code of offences has a chance for success.
The authors of the petition propose the new wording of Article 54a of the Code of Infractions.
It would read: "He who does not comply with the demand or order of a public official, issued within the borders and on the basis of the provisions of the Act, is subject to the penalty of arrest, fine or restriction of freedom."
"Par. 3 of this provision refers to an identical penalty for those who, without following instructions, will prevent or hinder the performance of their official activities," the journal reports.
Sociologist Andrzej Kowalski told “Rzeczpospolita” that although he agrees with the main message of the proposal, he fears that if it is implemented, it can be used in political matters of manifestations.
“In situations of libertarian demonstrations, this would be unfavorable and instead of mitigating the situation even to exacerbate it,” he adds.
According to statistics provided by the newspaper, 86 percent of citizens positively assessed police interventions in 2018 and that each month the services undertake 1.5 million interventions in the city.
Buras is worried about Europe
Jáchym Topol has writing in his blood.
Among his ancestors are editors, translators, writers.
His father, Josef Topol, was one of the most important post-war Czech playwrights, today mentioned in one row with Václav Havel.
On Jáchym, therefore, from the beginning hung a curse of talent, which he tries to use.
After eight years, he came back with “Sensitive Man.”
The protagonists are Buras and Soňa together with their two sons, who, however, do not form a typical Czech family.
They're wandering actors, traveling in Europe.
He's Shakespeare's lover and thief, she's a one-eyed translator who doesn't part with a bottle of wine.
One of the boys from birth did not make a sound, and for the other, time stopped in his infancy.
But it's not the heroes who are the most important in this story.
They are all only the background to observing a world in which there is no more room for artistic nomads.
It's the world of a flousy province, a society of old, suspicious people and promiscuous youth.
“Sensitive man” sometimes turns into an absurd theatricism.
The narrative mimics didascalia, and the conversations of the characters create this world.
The scene is Europe full of concerns about its future, but also constant questions about the past.
Belgium: Brussels Airlines cancels 105 flights due to Thomas Cook bankruptcy
Although the British travel agency Thomas Cook has collapsed, its Belgian subsidiary Neckermann is functioning.
However, the company has problems.
According to VRT television, Brussels Airlines has announced that it will cancel 105 flights in October due to problems related to the bankruptcy of Thomas Cook.
Neckermann also laid off 75 employees.
All trips with an office that have been booked in Belgium will be canceled, but holidaymakers will be able to receive a refund.
The court in Ghent also declared the bankruptness of three Belgian daughter companies: Thomas Cooking Belgium, Thomas Cooking Retail and Thomas Cook Financial Services.
Currently, about 12 thousand Belgians stay abroad on trips bought at Thomas Cook.
Another 40 thousand people were to leave with the office by the end of the year.
As recently as Monday, Thomas Coogg Belgium spokeswoman Leen Segers said the company is currently "fully operational," despite the British parent company's bankruptcy declaration, which caused concern among employees and customers.
At that time, the office reassured that the customers who bought trips in it would go on holiday.
Thomas Cook – the oldest and one of the world’s largest travel agents – declared bankruptcy on the night of Sunday to Monday.
The immediate cause of the bankruptcy was the collapse of last-chance talks with creditors and potential investors on Sunday.
In addition to the 900-million-pound rescue plan already agreed in August, the company will gain an additional 200 million as collateral for the winter period, when travel agencies usually have a lower turnover.
However, the company’s problems have been growing for several years, resulting in debt reaching 1.6 billion pounds.
