We're the ones who played very poorly.
This tournament is not over yet for us," said Polish volleyball captain Michał Kubiak after his defeat against Slovenia 1:3 in the semi-finals of the European Championship.
Görlitz in Germany offers free accommodation
Free accommodation in exchange for an opinion – such an extraordinary offer was prepared for newcomers German Görlitz.
The actions are intended to help in the fight against the progressive decline in the number of residents of the city.
Görlitz is Germany’s easternmost city.
The beautiful pastel old town attracts 140 thousand tourists every year.
However, as The Guardian writes, the city is facing considerable problems.
In Görlitz there are the lowest wages in Germany.
The city is also struggling with a permanent loss of population.
After the collapse of the Berlin Wall in 1989, most of them left for the west of the country, which translated into a 25 percent decline in the city's population.
In 2013, it amounted to 54 thousand people.
In order to fight the depopulation, officials came up with the idea of starting to offer free monthly accommodation in the city.
So far, 150 applications have been submitted.
Two-thirds of them were submitted by residents of large cities.
People from Hungary, the Czech Republic, the United States and the United Kingdom were also interested in the programme.
“When we applied, we thought that the program was supposed to convince people to move to Görlitz,” said one of the project’s participants.
"Now we feel like it doesn't matter if we stay here after four weeks or not," he added.
The city owns seven thousand vacancies, and unemployment is three times higher than the national average.
Although the city authorities hope that some will move permanently, the main goal of the program is to get the opinions of the participants of the program.
The information collected from the interviews and questionnaires is intended to help revive smaller German cities.
“We want to know more about what people need, and if they decide to move – about what their motivation was,” Robert Knippschild said.
Other cities have already experimented with ideas for luring new residents, offering them incentives in the form of accommodation.
The first was Detroit, which introduced a program to pay promising young professionals for living and working in the city for a year.
Today, such solutions are used in many places, from the Greek island of Antikythera to Candela in Italy, or in American Tulsa, Oklahoma.
